# Example kubeconfig placeholder — DO NOT PUT LIVE CREDENTIALS IN THE REPO
#
# This file is a sample showing where a kubeconfig would be located locally.
# Do NOT store real kubeconfigs or service-account tokens in the repository.
# Instead, add the base64-encoded kubeconfig to the GitHub Actions secret
# named `KUBE_CONFIG_DATA` and let workflows create the file at runtime.
#
# To create the secret locally (example):
#   $base = [Convert]::ToBase64String([System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes((Get-Content -Raw C:\path\to\your\kubeconfig)))
#   # then add $base as the `KUBE_CONFIG_DATA` repository secret
#
# Workflows in `.github/workflows/*.yml` already expect `KUBE_CONFIG_DATA`.
# If you need a local kubeconfig for testing, keep it outside the repo and
# add it to your personal environment or a dotfile ignored by git.

# --- replace with your local kubeconfig path in your environment, but DO NOT commit it
# kubeconfig: C:\Users\you\.kube\config
